            0.368,
            0.615
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_12_image_0.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.105,
            0.127,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "36"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.782,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "MATHEMATICS"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.139,
            0.9,
            0.196
          ],
          "content": "**Solution :** Let the ten’s and the unit’s digits in the first number be x and y, respectively. $\\mathrm { S o } ,$ , the first number may be written as $1 0 x + y$ in the expanded form (for example, $5 6 = 1 0 ( 5 ) + 6 )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.203,
            0.901,
            0.261
          ],
          "content": "When the digits are reversed, x becomes the unit’s digit and y becomes the ten’s digit. This number, in the expanded notation is $1 0 y   +   x$ (for example, when 56 is reversed, we get $6 5 = 1 0 ( 6 ) + 5 )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.266,
            0.415,
            0.285
          ],
          "content": "According to the given condition."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.265,
            0.291,
            0.524,
            0.309
          ],
          "content": "(10x + y) + (10y + x) = 66",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.315,
            0.138,
            0.332
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.381,
            0.314,
            0.524,
            0.333
          ],
          "content": "11(x + y) = 66",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.338,
            0.138,
            0.355
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.414,
            0.338,
            0.897,
            0.356
          ],
          "content": "x + y = 6\\tag{1}",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.361,
            0.616,
            0.38
          ],
          "content": "We are also given that the digits differ by 2, therefore,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.386,
            0.159,
            0.403
          ],
          "content": "either"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.415,
            0.386,
            0.897,
            0.404
          ],
          "content": "x - y = 2\\tag{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.412,
            0.125,
            0.425
          ],
          "content": "or"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.415,
            0.409,
            0.897,
            0.427
          ],
          "content": "y - x = 2\\tag{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.433,
            0.86,
            0.475
          ],
          "content": "If $x - y = 2$ , then solving (1) and (2) by elimination, we get $x = 4$ and $y = 2$ In this case, we get the number 42."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.102,
            0.48,
            0.872,
            0.524
          ],
          "content": "If $y - x = 2$ , then solving (1) and (3) by elimination, we get $x = 2$ and $y = 4$ In this case, we get the number 24."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.531,
            0.53,
            0.549
          ],
          "content": "Thus, there are two such numbers 42 and 24."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.555,
            0.87,
            0.574
          ],
          "content": "**Verification :** Here $42+24=66$ and $4 - 2 = 2$ . Also $24 + 42 = 66$ and $4 - 2 = 2$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.41,
            0.593,
            0.588,
            0.612
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "EXERCISE 3.3"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.623,
            0.899,
            0.726
          ],
          "content": "**1.** Solve the following pair of linear equations by the elimination method and the substitution method : (i) $x + y = 5$ and $2x - 3y = 4$ (ii) $3x + 4y = 10$ and $2x - 2y = 2$ (iii) $3x-5y-4=0 \\quad  and  \\quad 9x=2y+7$ (iv) $\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{2y}{3} = -1   and   x - \\frac{y}{3} = 3$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.736,
            0.899,
            0.772
          ],
          "content": "**2.** Form the pair of linear equations in the following problems, and find their solutions (if they exist) by the elimination method :"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.17,
            0.777,
            0.898,
            0.841
          ],
          "content": "(i) If we add 1 to the numerator and subtract 1 from the denominator, a fraction reduces to 1. It becomes $\\frac { 1 } { 2 }$ if we only add 1 to the denominator. What is the fraction?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.847,
            0.9,
            0.881
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Five years ago, Nuri was thrice as old as Sonu. Ten years later, Nuri will be twice as old as Sonu. How old are Nuri and Sonu?"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.967,
            0.563,
            0.982
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.885,
            0.901,
            0.919
          ],
          "content": "(iii) The sum of the digits of a two-digit number is 9. Also, nine times this number is twice the number obtained by reversing the order of the digits. Find the number."
        }
      ]
    }
